= The Four Foundations =
== Ontology · Epistemology · Axiology · Praxeology ==

''A thing may exist without being known; be known without being valued; be valued without being pursued; and be pursued without being understood.''

'''Ontology''' studies being and existence: what is real and what it means for something to exist. '''Epistemology''' studies knowledge: what can be known and what justifies our beliefs. '''Axiology''' studies value: what is good, beautiful, worthy, or significant. '''Praxeology''' studies purposeful action: how agents pursue ends, employ means, and produce consequences.

Each discipline addresses a different problem. Ontology concerns the reality about which knowledge makes claims. Epistemology examines whether those claims are justified. Axiology investigates the worth of what is encountered, understood, or pursued. Praxeology examines what it means to act upon the world.

Within these distinctions lie some of philosophy's oldest and most consequential problems.

What does it mean for something to exist? What transforms belief into knowledge? Does worth belong to things themselves, or arise through judgment and relationship? What distinguishes an action from an occurrence, and what makes its consequences attributable to an agent?

These questions concern more than four branches of philosophical inquiry. They expose four irreducible dimensions of our encounter with reality: '''being, knowing, valuing, and acting'''.

We find ourselves in a world whose existence precedes our understanding of it. We form beliefs about that world through faculties whose reliability we must themselves investigate. We encounter things we desire, admire, fear, preserve, or reject, without always knowing what makes them worthy of those responses. And we intervene in circumstances we cannot completely foresee, creating consequences that become part of the reality we must subsequently understand.

Every answer invites another question. Existence does not guarantee intelligibility. Knowledge does not establish worth. Value does not produce action. Action does not guarantee wisdom.

Ontology, epistemology, axiology, and praxeology arise from these distinctions. Each investigates a fundamental problem that the others cannot resolve on its behalf.

----
== I. Ontology — The Problem of Being ==

'''Ontology is the branch of metaphysics concerned with being, existence, and the fundamental categories of reality.''' It asks what exists, what kinds of things exist, what makes them what they are, and how they persist, change, depend upon, or relate to one another.

Its importance is fundamental: before we can claim to know something, determine its worth, or act upon it, we must have some conception of what that thing is. Yet ontology alone cannot establish whether our conceptions are justified, whether what exists is valuable, or what we ought to do.

Ontology examines the difference between '''reality and our descriptions of reality'''. An ontology may classify the world, but a classification is not automatically a discovery about the world's fundamental structure. The categories through which we think may reflect genuine distinctions, human conventions, or some combination of both.

For an introduction to the discipline and its relationship to the wider field, see the [https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/metaphysics/ Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy's treatment of metaphysics].

Before anything can be explained, valued, or transformed, there is the question of what there is.

Ontology investigates being: existence and nonexistence, identity and difference, substance and process, possibility and necessity, causation and relation.

Its questions are deceptively elementary.

What is a thing? What makes it the thing it is? What distinguishes an entity from its properties, its parts, or the processes through which it changes? Can something remain itself while becoming something different? Do possibilities have any kind of existence? Are relationships secondary connections between independently existing things, or are some things constituted by their relationships?

These are not merely problems of language. They concern the fundamental structure of reality.

=== Being and Nonbeing ===

Philosophy has long confronted the difficulty of speaking about what is not.

We can describe an absent object, imagine an unreal creature, contemplate a possible future, or deny the existence of something. Yet the capacity to think and speak about these things does not establish that they exist in the same manner as the physical world.

The distinction between being and nonbeing opens questions about negation, absence, fiction, possibility, and the limits of existence.

A mathematical object, a physical organism, a social institution, and an imagined character may all become objects of thought. Whether they exist, and in what sense, requires further argument.

Ontology must distinguish the existence of a representation from the existence of what it represents.

A description can be coherent without being true of the world. An entity can exist without having been described. Our conceptual boundaries do not necessarily coincide with the boundaries of reality.

The question also concerns '''ontological commitment''': what must be admitted as existing for a theory or description of reality to be true?

A theory may refer to numbers, forces, minds, species, institutions, possibilities, or fictional characters. But reference alone does not settle whether these entities possess independent existence.

To ask what a theory presupposes is different from asking whether those presuppositions are warranted.

The first question is ontological. The second enters epistemology.

=== Substance and Process ===

One of ontology's enduring tensions concerns whether reality is fundamentally composed of things that undergo change or processes from which relatively stable things emerge.

[https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/parmenides/ Parmenides of Elea] challenged the intelligibility of change and nonbeing. [https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/heraclitus/ Heraclitus of Ephesus] became associated with the primacy of becoming and flux. Their contrasting positions exposed a difficulty that continues to shape metaphysics: how can reality be both intelligibly stable and perpetually changing?

Aristotle's ''first philosophy'' investigates beings insofar as they are beings, seeking their causes and principles at the most fundamental level. His distinctions between substance, form, matter, potentiality, and actuality offer a sophisticated account of persistence and transformation.

These arguments are developed in [https://classics.mit.edu/Aristotle/metaphysics.html Aristotle's ''Metaphysics''], particularly Books IV, VII, and IX. Contemporary scholarship further examines their implications in [https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/aristotle-metaphysics/ ''Aristotle's Metaphysics''].

A living organism changes its material composition while retaining a recognizable identity. A river persists as a river though its waters continually pass. A society can retain its name and institutions while its members, laws, customs, and relationships transform.

What, precisely, persists?

A substance-oriented metaphysics seeks an account of enduring entities and their changing properties. Process philosophies, developed in different forms and notably by Alfred North Whitehead, challenge the assumption that stable things are always more fundamental than events, activities, and becoming.

Whitehead's [https://www.simonandschuster.com/books/Process-and-Reality/Alfred-North-Whitehead/9780029345702 ''Process and Reality'' (1929)] develops a metaphysical system in which actual occasions—events of becoming—occupy a fundamental role. The broader tradition and its disagreements are surveyed in [https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/process-philosophy/ ''Process Philosophy''].

Neither perspective can be dismissed by merely observing that things change or that some identities endure. Both must explain the apparent coexistence of continuity and transformation.

=== Identity, Possibility, and Causation ===

Ontology extends beyond the identification of existing objects.

It investigates what makes an entity identical to itself across time, whether properties are essential or contingent, and what distinguishes what is actual from what is merely possible.

Could an entity have existed differently while remaining the same entity? What makes something necessary rather than contingent? Does causation describe a fundamental connection in reality or a structure we infer from the regularities we observe?

These questions open the field of '''modality''': the philosophical investigation of possibility, necessity, contingency, and impossibility.

That something happens does not make it necessary. That something has not happened does not establish that it is impossible.

A further problem concerns '''metaphysical dependence'''.

Can one thing exist only because another exists? Are composite objects dependent upon their parts? Are mental states dependent upon physical processes? Do social institutions depend upon collective recognition?

Theories of grounding attempt to explain how some facts or entities obtain in virtue of others, without simply reducing every form of dependence to physical causation.

Questions of dependence and emergence complicate the picture further.

An organism depends on its constituents without being adequately understood as a mere inventory of them. An institution depends on persons, practices, rules, and recognition, yet may exert causal influence upon the very individuals who sustain it.

Whether such examples establish genuinely emergent entities, reducible configurations, or some other ontological relation remains contested.

Ontology neither requires that everything be reduced to elementary substances nor licenses the declaration that everything is fundamentally process.

It demands that such positions be articulated, examined, and defended.

'''The problem of being is not simply to enumerate what exists, but to understand what existence entails—and what the world must be like for identity, change, possibility, and relation to be intelligible.'''

----
== II. Epistemology — The Problem of Knowing ==

'''Epistemology is the philosophical study of knowledge and justified belief.''' It investigates what knowledge is, how it is acquired, what distinguishes it from opinion or fortunate guessing, and whether its claims can be justified.

Its importance lies in the gap between '''reality and what we believe about reality'''. The existence of something does not guarantee that we know it exists. Even a true belief may fail to qualify as knowledge if its truth is accidental or its grounds are defective.

Where ontology investigates what exists, epistemology investigates our access to it. Its central concern is not simply the collection of evidence, but the conditions that make knowledge possible.

A contemporary overview is provided by the [https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/epistemology/ Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy's ''Epistemology''] and [https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/knowledge-analysis/ ''The Analysis of Knowledge''].

We encounter reality through perception, memory, reason, testimony, inference, and experience. Yet each of these can mislead us.

We remember events incorrectly. We perceive appearances that conceal their causes. We reason from mistaken premises. We trust authorities who themselves may be mistaken. Even a conviction reached through apparently sound methods can turn out to be false.

Epistemology begins with this predicament.

It investigates the nature of knowledge, its sources and justification, the conditions of truth and belief, and the limits of what can be known.

Its problem is not simply how to accumulate evidence. It is how evidence, experience, and reasoning can place us in a genuine relation of knowledge to the world.

=== Knowledge and Belief ===

Belief and knowledge are not interchangeable.

We can believe what is false. We can correctly believe something without knowing why it is true. We can possess strong reasons for a belief that nevertheless fails.

A traditional analysis characterizes propositional knowledge as ''justified true belief''. To know that something is the case, the proposition must be true, the person must believe it, and the belief must possess appropriate justification.

This concerns '''propositional knowledge'''—knowledge that a statement is true.

It does not exhaust every sense of knowing.

Knowing how to perform a skill, knowing a person through acquaintance, and understanding why something happens raise related but distinguishable epistemological questions.

The distinction matters because an account adequate for factual propositions may not fully explain practical knowledge, familiarity, or understanding.

Yet even the familiar account of propositional knowledge is insufficient.

In [https://academic.oup.com/analysis/article-abstract/23/6/121/109949 “Is Justified True Belief Knowledge?” (1963)], philosopher Edmund Gettier presented cases in which a belief is both justified and true but nevertheless fails to constitute knowledge.

Gettier's examples demonstrated that a belief may be justified and true while its truth still depends on luck. The justification can support the belief without connecting the believer to the fact in the right way.

The difficulty is profound.

If having evidence and reaching a true conclusion do not always amount to knowledge, what additional relation is required?

Some theories appeal to reliable processes. Others emphasize intellectual virtues, the exclusion of relevant alternatives, or the absence of factors that defeat justification. Still others question whether knowledge can be adequately reconstructed from simpler components at all.

These approaches include '''reliabilism''', which examines the reliability of belief-forming processes; '''virtue epistemology''', which examines the role of intellectual abilities and excellences; and approaches that seek to exclude the particular kinds of epistemic luck exposed by Gettier's examples.

The distinction between being right and knowing is therefore not a technicality. It is central to the possibility of responsible inquiry.

=== Skepticism and the Regress of Reasons ===

Every justification invites a question: ''Why should that justification be accepted?''

An assertion may be supported by evidence, but the credibility of that evidence may depend on further assumptions. Those assumptions may require additional justification, and so on.

Where does this sequence end?

This is the '''regress problem of justification'''.

Foundationalism seeks beliefs or sources of justification that do not depend upon further beliefs in the ordinary way. Coherentism investigates justification through the relations among beliefs within a system. Infinitist approaches allow an indefinitely extended structure of reasons.

These alternatives offer different answers to the problem of how justificatory support is possible. They are examined in the [https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/justep-foundational/ Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy's discussion of foundationalist theories of epistemic justification].

None escapes philosophical difficulties merely by naming its preferred structure.

Radical skepticism presses the challenge further. If our senses sometimes deceive us, what establishes their general reliability? If reasoning evaluates reasoning, how can circularity be avoided? If every claim is open to doubt, can any claim be known?

René Descartes transformed these difficulties into a search for certainty in [https://www.earlymoderntexts.com/assets/pdfs/descartes1641.pdf ''Meditations on First Philosophy'' (1641)].

David Hume exposed the problem of justifying inductive expectations from past experience, particularly in [https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/9662 ''An Enquiry Concerning Human Understanding'' (1748)].

Immanuel Kant asked about the conditions that make experience and knowledge possible in [https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/kant/ ''Critique of Pure Reason'' (1781; revised 1787)].

Their projects differ substantially, but each confronts the relationship between the world and the faculties through which we apprehend it.

Kant's question is particularly significant: rather than only asking what we know, he investigates what structures of sensibility and understanding must be present for certain kinds of experience and knowledge to occur.

This ''transcendental'' approach differs from simply gathering further evidence within an already accepted account of experience.

=== The Limits of the Knower ===

Knowledge is acquired under conditions.

An observer occupies a position. An instrument has a range of sensitivity. A method rests on assumptions. A model excludes features of the reality it attempts to represent.

Recognizing these limitations does not entail that reality is merely subjective or that every perspective is equally justified.

Rather, it requires distinguishing what is observed from what is inferred, what is demonstrated from what is plausible, and what remains uncertain from what has been established.

The possibility of error is not an argument against knowledge. It is one reason knowledge requires criticism.

Epistemic humility, properly understood, is neither intellectual surrender nor the refusal to reach conclusions. It is the discipline of holding convictions in proportion to their justification while remaining capable of revision.

A related position, '''fallibilism''', holds that some knowledge can be genuine even when the knower lacks an absolute guarantee against error.

Fallibilism should not be confused with the claim that truth itself changes according to our beliefs. The truth of a proposition and the certainty with which it is held are different matters.

Certainty may be possible in some domains and unattainable in others. Mathematical demonstration, empirical investigation, historical reconstruction, and philosophical argument do not establish their conclusions through identical procedures.

Epistemology must investigate these differences without assuming that one form of justification can replace every other.

'''The problem of knowing is not merely whether our beliefs are true, but what makes their truth something we know rather than something we happen to believe.'''

